--TEST--
`$uri->resolve` answers RFC 3986 § 5.4.2's abnormal examples: an over-long ascent stops at the root, a dot segment is a whole segment, and one inside a query or fragment is data
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Uri::toString
// The base is the RFC's own, so every row below is RFC 3986 § 5.4's table verbatim
// rather than a shape chosen here.
var $b = Core\Uri::parse("http://a/b/c/d;p?q");

// RFC 3986 § 5.4.1's ascent ladder is the bound the abnormal rows sit past: an empty
// reference is the base itself (without its fragment), `.` keeps the directory
// and each `..` walks up one segment.
echo $b->resolved("")->toString(), "|", $b->resolved(".")->toString(), "\n";
echo $b->resolved("..")->toString(), "|", $b->resolved("../..")->toString(), "\n";

// RFC 3986 § 5.4.2: an ascent past the root is neither an error nor an escape — it
// stops at the root, however many segments are left over. That is the other
// side of the ladder above, and it is why a leading `/../` is harmless too.
echo $b->resolved("../../../g")->toString(), "|",
     $b->resolved("../../../../g")->toString(), "\n";
echo $b->resolved("/./g")->toString(), "|", $b->resolved("/../g")->toString(), "\n";

// RFC 3986 § 5.4.2: only a *complete* segment is a dot segment, so a name that merely
// begins or ends with a dot is an ordinary one and is kept.
echo $b->resolved("g.")->toString(), "|", $b->resolved(".g")->toString(), "|",
     $b->resolved("g..")->toString(), "|", $b->resolved("..g")->toString(), "\n";
echo $b->resolved("./../g")->toString(), "|", $b->resolved("./g/.")->toString(), "\n";
echo $b->resolved("g/./h")->toString(), "|", $b->resolved("g/../h")->toString(), "\n";
echo $b->resolved("g;x=1/./y")->toString(), "|", $b->resolved("g;x=1/../y")->toString(), "\n";

// RFC 3986 § 5.4.2: the removal is over the *path* only, so the identical text inside a
// query or a fragment is data and survives untouched.
echo $b->resolved("g?y/./x")->toString(), "|", $b->resolved("g?y/../x")->toString(), "\n";
echo $b->resolved("g#s/./x")->toString(), "|", $b->resolved("g#s/../x")->toString(), "\n";

// RFC 3986 § 5.4.2's last row is the one an implementation may answer two
// ways. This is the strict reading RFC 3986 § 5.2.2 asks for: a reference that names a scheme is its
// own URI, and is not merged into the base whose scheme happens to match.
echo $b->resolved("http:g")->toString(), "\n";

// RFC 3986 § 5.2.1 makes the receiver the base, so it has to be absolute;
// RFC 3986 § 5.2.3 merges
// a relative reference into a path, so an opaque base has nowhere to merge it.
try {
    echo Core\Uri::parse("/relative/base")->resolved("g")->toString(), "\n";
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    echo "relative: ", $e->message, "\n";
}
try {
    echo Core\Uri::parse("mailto:a@b")->resolved("g")->toString(), "\n";
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    echo "opaque: ", $e->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
http://a/b/c/d;p?q|http://a/b/c/
http://a/b/|http://a/
http://a/g|http://a/g
http://a/g|http://a/g
http://a/b/c/g.|http://a/b/c/.g|http://a/b/c/g..|http://a/b/c/..g
http://a/b/g|http://a/b/c/g/
http://a/b/c/g/h|http://a/b/c/h
http://a/b/c/g;x=1/y|http://a/b/c/y
http://a/b/c/g?y/./x|http://a/b/c/g?y/../x
http://a/b/c/g#s/./x|http://a/b/c/g#s/../x
http:g
relative: Core\Uri::resolved(): the receiver is a relative reference, and RFC 3986 § 5.2.1 resolves against an absolute URI. Give this `Uri` a scheme first, or resolve against one that has one
opaque: Core\Uri::resolved(): the base has no authority and a rootless path, so it is opaque and there is no path for a relative reference to be merged into
